    {
      "id": "claim_674508e03cec4549a35bdee414d792de",
      "statement": "internal/subscription's package doc states the entire multi-profile design rests on one confirmed fact: every supported vendor CLI selects its configuration directory from an environment variable, and many-ai-cli only ever creates a directory and sets that one variable, never reading, writing, parsing, or storing the credential itself.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/subscription/adapter.go#L1-L9",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0d062c986dd6bd6db5cdcf5c0f27645448f7b1a0702f1756483d1f59757f1274:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e981afbc10e04d29bf97d221cfc2d761",
      "statement": "A spawn with no subscription profile selected must produce a byte-for-byte identical environment to an unmodified spawn (enforced by TestSubscriptionLaunchWithoutProfileLeavesEnvUnchanged), and a live session's credentials are never swapped mid-session, enforced by TestLiveSessionAuthIsNeverSwapped scanning the source for such an assignment rather than relying only on a runtime test.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/subscription/adapter.go#L21-L27",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:9e05174ad19294963a876e4a9b500cdec7064b8ad38f6c0bd5f6b75618e9d864:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_229c7b55f0cb40d4aa43af4469ec19b8",
      "statement": "internal/subscription's package doc states remaining usage quota cannot be queried from any vendor CLI (measured 2026-08-17), so there is deliberately no ReadUsage method on the provider Adapter interface; this is stated as distinct from whether usage data exists at all, since three separate local-file data paths do exist and are read by the Hub.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/subscription/adapter.go#L28-L37",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e8facbee4f083b8a15305957e2902c3c83d605945dee6eef855cd15f414bdc17:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_1094e2ce83b442c8a4bc1f6b2e022c42",
      "statement": "seed.go's doc states three rules bound what a subscription profile inherits: entries are carried in only additively (never renaming or deleting something already present, with the one named exception that SeedSyncFile re-reads the policy keys of the vendor settings file from the default on every pass while vendor-written state keys stay the profile's), every seed write lands only under ~/.many-ai-cli/subscriptions/ so the user's real ~/.claude/~/.codex/~/.grok are read but never written, and each provider adapter lists exactly which entries to carry by name rather than copying an entire directory wholesale.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/subscription/seed.go#L34-L51",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5d31087c7f97413c217ef8d4e5962b2c8b16d0e1e5809a0cf3dd7415ab74f1ca:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_be4a8db5fbf94be1be3e4b10ba89f754",
      "statement": "SeedCopyFile copies a file once as-is for files the vendor CLI itself rewrites, because a symlink there would push the profile's own edits back into the user's default configuration; SeedLinkDir instead links a whole directory (symlink, or a junction on Windows) for content the user maintains in one place, such as skills, slash commands, and prompts, so a later addition reaches every profile without a re-seed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://internal/subscription/seed.go#L53-L69",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5a1a81d7dc986ea17b350b47e44aec5858842e597288ee0bb325b4f685bb496b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f541f046fc1542a5aeb5ff65f6ae48f2",
      "statement": "usagerelay's package doc states a hard security requirement: only numeric metadata (token counts, cost, model name, elapsed time) from Claude's statusLine hook or Codex's Stop hook is ever sent to the Hub's /api/session-usage endpoint; for Codex's rollout JSONL specifically, only the numeric fields of token_count events are extracted and conversation-body lines are read and discarded without ever being held in memory, and an HTTP send failure logs a stderr warning but still exits 0 so the vendor CLI's own hook is never broken.",
